Q: BroadcastExchange breaks RDD&#39;s lineage A: <p>I have looked into the behavior of <code>cleanShuffleDependencies</code> when a broadcast exchange is triggered, and I would like to share my perspective on the issue to help add the missing pieces.</p>
<p><strong>1. Regarding your first question, your assumption that the broadcast exchange cuts the lineage is confirmed.</strong></p>
<p>According to the <a href="https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/scala/org/apache/spark/broadcast/Broadcast.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">official Apache Spark documentation</a>, a broadcast variable allows the programmer to keep a read-only variable cached on each machine rather than shipping a copy of it with tasks. It acts as a wrapper around the original value. Because it does not ship with standard tasks, it bypasses standard RDD dependency links like <code>NarrowDependency</code> or <code>ShuffleDependency</code>.</p>
<p>When a <code>BroadcastExchange</code> triggers in Spark SQL, it implements this exact concept. It executes an independent job to collect all data to the Driver, then uses Broadcast to push this data directly into the memory of all Executors. The downstream RDD simply accesses this cached data via the wrapper's <code>.value</code> method rather than traversing back to a parent RDD, cutting the lineage connection chain at this point.</p>
<p>Based on the <a href="https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/master/sql/core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/execution/exchange/BroadcastExchangeExec.scala#L253" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>BroadcastExchangeExec.scala</code> source code</a>, this mechanism of breaking the RDD graph is demonstrated through two specific operations:</p>
<p>First (from lines 253 to 255), this node blocks the formation of lineage by throwing an exception if Spark calls the <code>doExecute()</code> function to create an RDD link:</p>
<pre class="lang-scala prettyprint-override"><code>override protected def doExecute(): RDD[InternalRow] = {
  throw QueryExecutionErrors.executeCodePathUnsupportedError(&quot;BroadcastExchange&quot;)
}
</code></pre>
<p>Next (from lines 176 to 246), instead of maintaining an RDD connection, the data is collected directly to the Driver via the <code>executeCollectIterator()</code> function and immediately broadcast through memory as an asynchronous broadcast variable (<code>relationFuture</code>):</p>
<pre><code>@transient
override lazy val relationFuture: Future[broadcast.Broadcast[Any]] = {
  SQLExecution.withThreadLocalCaptured[broadcast.Broadcast[Any]](
    session, BroadcastExchangeExec.executionContext) {
      // ...
      // Use executeCollect/executeCollectIterator to avoid conversion to Scala types
      val (numRows, input) = child.executeCollectIterator()
      // ...
      // Construct the relation.
      val relation = mode.transform(input, Some(numRows))
      // ...
      val broadcasted = sparkContext.broadcastInternal(relation, serializedOnly = true)
      // ...
      promise.trySuccess(broadcasted)
      broadcasted
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>With this mechanism, the main query waits for <code>relationFuture</code> to complete. The main RDD graph does not retain information about the stages that created the broadcast data. Therefore, when the <code>cleanShuffleDependencies</code> function traverses the lineage backward, it stops at the exchange point.</p>
<p><strong>2. Regarding your second question, the reliance on a broadcast variable is not visible within the RDD lineage graph.</strong></p>
<p>The RDD graph (the output of <code>.toDebugString</code>) maps data partitioning dependencies between RDDs. Because a broadcast variable is an object in distributed memory rather than a parent RDD, this dependency is encapsulated as a closure variable within the Java code generated by <code>WholeStageCodegen</code>.</p>
<p>To observe the exact location of the broadcast and its upstream dependencies, check the SQL Physical Plan using the <code>.explain()</code> command instead of <code>.toDebugString</code>:</p>
<pre><code>// RDD lineage hides the broadcast operation and upstream dependencies
println(df.rdd.toDebugString)

// Physical plan displays the BroadcastExchange node
df.explain(&quot;physical&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>If you need to inspect or clear shuffle dependencies created prior to the broadcast, you must intercept the <code>DataFrame</code> right before it gets consumed by the broadcast operation (for example, just before a join). Calling <code>.rdd</code> at that specific stage allows you to execute your logic safely.</p>
<p>Feel free to point out any mistakes or share further thoughts in the comments.
If you find this answer somewhat helpful, please give it an upvote!</p>

Q: How do I make my website change layouts depending on screen size? A: <p>Have you tried @media queries? for ex:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@media (max-width: 700px) {
    form {
        width: 92%;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Alternatively, you can also use relative sizes like rem and/or clamp elements:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.2rem, 1.4rem) 
</code></pre>
<p>You can also use JavaScript to apply specific classes based on screen size, however I don't recommend this.</p>
<p>it depends on what your website needs, I use both every site as needed.</p>
<p>If I misunderstood your question or you needed something else, feel free to ask me</p>
